# Seven notes: five shown to the default reader (minisuperspace, axions, bra-ket wormholes, Picard-Lefschetz, KSW),
# two for students only. One sits in the introduction (saddles and horizon entropy, which sect-1.intro-p3 assumes);
# the minisuperspace, bra-ket and Picard-Lefschetz notes sit in section 1.2, where the argument first uses them.
# Listed in reading order.
BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-saddles", "after": "sect-1.intro-p3", "title": "Euclidean saddles and horizon entropy", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A thermal partition function $\operatorname{Tr}\,e^{-\beta H}$ is a path integral in imaginary time, periodic with period $\beta$. In gravity Gibbons and Hawking evaluated it by its classical solutions, the saddles: each contributes about $e^{-I}$, with $I$ the Euclidean action of the solution, and the free energy and entropy follow. For a black hole the Euclidean solution is smooth only at one temperature, and its action gives the Bekenstein–Hawking entropy, a quarter of the horizon area in Planck units. Wick-rotating de Sitter space gives a round sphere; its action is $-A/4G$, with $A$ the area of the cosmological horizon, so the sphere's weight is $e^{A/4G}$. If the sphere computes a trace, this is $e^S$, with $S$ the entropy of the static patch. The paper takes this entropy as given and asks which sum over geometries reproduces it.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-minisuperspace", "after": "sect1.2proposal-p1", "title": "Minisuperspace and the lapse", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In the ADM form of general relativity the metric is split into spatial slices, a lapse $N$ that sets how much time elapses between neighbouring slices, and a shift. The lapse and shift appear without time derivatives: they are Lagrange multipliers, and integrating over them imposes the Hamiltonian and momentum constraints. Minisuperspace keeps only homogeneous geometries, here $ds^2=N^2d\tau^2+a(\tau)^2d\Omega^2$ with $0\le\tau\le1$, so the lapse is a single number, the total elapsed time. The path integral then becomes the quantum mechanics of one particle, the scale factor $a$, propagated for a time $N$ and integrated over $N$. Real $N$ gives Euclidean histories and imaginary $N$ Lorentzian ones, so the contour of the $N$ integral decides which geometries are summed. That contour is the freedom the paper uses to define the gravitational path integral.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-axions", "after": "sect2.1setup-p1", "title": "Why axions support Euclidean wormholes", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In a closed Euclidean universe the Friedmann equation reads $\dot a^2/a^2=1/a^2-H^2+(\text{energy term})$, and a throat, a minimum of $a$ with $\dot a=0$ and $\ddot a>0$, requires negative Euclidean energy density. An ordinary scalar cannot provide it. An axion can: in four dimensions it is dual to a three-form field strength, and the dual description, natural when the flux through the spatial sphere is fixed, has an opposite-sign kinetic term in Euclidean signature. This is how Giddings and Strominger built Euclidean axion wormholes. In three dimensions the field strength is a two-form, a flux $q$ through the spatial two-sphere, which adds a term growing like $q^2/a^2$ at small size. In the particle picture this is a wall near $a=0$, so Euclidean solutions bounce and can be strung into necklaces.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-braket", "after": "configuration-1-tunneling-solutions-p2", "title": "Bra-ket wormholes", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A state prepared by a gravitational path integral, such as the no-boundary state, has a bra and a ket, each produced by its own geometry. Chen, Gorbenko and Maldacena noticed that the path integral computing an expectation value can also include geometries connecting the bra's preparation to the ket's: bra-ket wormholes. In cosmology they typically pass through a region where the metric is Lorentzian or complex, and they can avoid a big-bang or big-crunch singularity by going around it in the complex plane of time or of the scale factor. Such configurations are not real Euclidean metrics, so whether they should be counted is settled by a contour prescription or by an allowability criterion, not by the geometry alone. In this paper the dominant necklace contains one: its Lorentzian piece carries the universe through zero size, from $a$ to $-a$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-picard", "after": "configuration-1-tunneling-solutions-p3", "title": "Picard–Lefschetz theory", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>An integral $\int_\mathcal C dN\,e^{-I(N)}$ with holomorphic $I$ can be evaluated by deforming the contour $\mathcal C$ in the complex plane. Through each saddle $\sigma$, where $I'=0$, run two curves of constant phase, $\text{Im}\,I$ constant: the steepest-descent curve, a Lefschetz thimble, along which $\text{Re}\,I$ grows away from the saddle, and the steepest-ascent curve, along which it falls. Picard–Lefschetz theory states that $\mathcal C$ equals a sum of thimbles with integer coefficients, the coefficient of each being the number of times its steepest-ascent curve crosses $\mathcal C$. Saddles with coefficient zero do not contribute, however small their action. Poles of $I$ add endpoints and can change which crossings occur. In this paper the question “which necklaces contribute?” becomes which steepest-ascent curves cross the lapse contour.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-nohair", "after": "sect-3.3-p2", "title": "The cosmic no-hair theorem", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In an expanding universe with a positive cosmological constant, the energy densities of ordinary matter and radiation, of spatial curvature and of anisotropy all dilute as the universe grows, while the vacuum energy does not. Wald proved for homogeneous models, and Starobinsky argued more generally, that such a universe therefore approaches de Sitter space at late times, whatever it started with: it loses its “hair”. For a closed universe with axion flux, the flux term falls off as the scale factor grows, and the solution at late Lorentzian time is de Sitter space up to a shift of time. Physical quantities that can be computed at late times, such as an action evaluated on a contour deformed there, then cannot remember the flux. The paper invokes this cosmic no-hair theorem to explain why the dominant necklace's action is $-\pi/2G$ for every $q$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-ksw", "after": "sect5-concl-p5", "title": "The KSW criterion for complex metrics", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Saddles of the gravitational path integral are often complex metrics, and not every complex metric can be allowed: some would make ordinary matter's path integral diverge. Kontsevich and Segal, followed by Witten, proposed a criterion. A complex metric is allowable if, at every point, the quadratic form $g_{\mu\nu}$ can be diagonalised with eigenvalues whose absolute arguments sum to less than $\pi$, which makes the action of every $p$-form field have positive real part. Real Euclidean metrics pass trivially, and Lorentzian metrics sit on the boundary. The criterion has been used to rule out saddles in no-boundary cosmology. A weaker “spectral” version asks only that one-loop partition functions of matter on the background be well defined. The paper's detour around the crunch fails the first and may pass the second.</p>"""},
]
